.TH BOOTIQUE 1 "" "bootique" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bootique \- boot-cosmetics suite: a GRUB menu theme + a Plymouth boot splash
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./setup.sh
.RB [ install | uninstall | check | test | version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
bootique is a boot-cosmetics suite: a GRUB menu theme and a Plymouth boot splash
that share one photo and one palette, so a machine looks like itself from
power-on to login. It is a THEME PACK WITH A SYSTEM INSTALLER: unlike a
.IR ~/.local
gadget, its
.B setup.sh
installs to
.I /boot/grub/themes/bootique
and
.I /usr/share/plymouth/themes/bootique
with sudo and rebuilds the initramfs and
.IR grub.cfg .
.PP
The install is idempotent: every piece is cmp/size/stamp-gated, so a settled box
is a no-op and the initramfs and grub.cfg regenerate only when something
changed. A box lacking GRUB or lacking Plymouth skips that half; a missing
optional dependency (grub-mkfont, ImageMagick, a TTF) degrades with a note.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Place the GRUB theme (background, generated fonts, the selection bar + frame,
theme.txt, the GRUB_THEME drop-in, quiet_boot) and the Plymouth theme (the theme
files, the default.plymouth alternative, the quiet-splash drop-in, early-KMS),
then regenerate the initramfs and grub.cfg as needed.
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the drop-ins and both theme dirs and regenerate, reverting to a plain
boot.
.TP
.B check
Audit every managed piece; emit [OK]/[FAIL]/[WARN] markers and a non-zero exit
on drift.
.TP
.B test
Run the in-repo stub suite (no root; nothing on the box is touched).
.SH UNLOCK PROMPT
Where a network auto-unlock (clevis/Tang) exists it races the human for the same
passphrase request, so the passphrase pill is live from the first frame in
either mode and the block around it carries the state.
.TP
.I racing
The auto-unlock owns the headline ("Unlocking automatically over the home
network") with a progress track filling one cell per second under it, and the
pill sits below, labelled "or type your passphrase". A boot waiting on the
network says so prominently instead of burying it in a caption.
.TP
.I required
Amber: the window closed and nothing answered. Reached on a ~20s timer, so the
racing copy can never linger on a box whose anchor is unreachable.
.TP
.I rejected
Amber, under the pill: the last answer was refused.
.PP
Once the root filesystem is mounted the prompt is LATCHED SHUT for the rest of
the boot. Plymouth replays its state when a late-loading GPU driver brings up a
new renderer, and without the latch that replay counted as a second attempt,
putting an "incorrect passphrase" pill over a boot that had already succeeded.
.PP
The Plymouth script language can read neither a file nor the kernel command
line, so the mode is BAKED INTO THE THEME at install time.
.B remote
means expect a network unlock attempt;
.B local
means never attempt and never imply one, so such a box shows no racing state, no
track and no claim about a home network. It is autodetected
(is clevis present on this box?) unless overridden by
.B BS_UNLOCK_MODE
.RB ( local | remote | auto ,
this run) or
.I /etc/bootique/unlock-mode
(persistent, and visible to
.B check
as an environment variable would not be).
.B check
compares the installed theme against what this box should have, so gaining or
losing an auto-unlock reads as drift. Pin the file on any box whose answer must
not depend on detection: autodetection asks the cheap, sudo-free question (does
this box carry clevis?), which is not the same as whether the disk is actually
bound to it.
.SH EARLY-KMS
Forces the GPU driver + firmware into the initramfs so the panel is at native
resolution before Plymouth's first frame (the post-unlock resize fix). A dracut
.I force_drivers
drop-in on a dracut box with an
.B xe
GPU, the initramfs-tools hook otherwise, nothing on a non-xe box. It composes
with other initramfs drop-ins (e.g. an early-boot ssh server for remote unlock)
via
.BR update-initramfs .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /boot/grub/themes/bootique/
The installed GRUB theme (background, *.pf2 fonts, sel_*/frame_* slices,
theme.txt).
.TP
.I /usr/share/plymouth/themes/bootique/
The installed Plymouth theme (bootique.plymouth, bootique.script, the PNGs).
.TP
.I /etc/default/grub.d/{bootique,splash}.cfg
The GRUB_THEME and quiet-splash drop-ins.
.TP
.I /etc/bootique/unlock-mode
Optional persistent unlock-mode override, one word:
.B local
or
.BR remote .
Written by an operator or a provisioning layer; bootique reads it and never
creates or removes it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR plymouth (8),
.BR grub-mkconfig (8),
.BR update-initramfs (8)
